  "output" : "Confirmed — the description matches the implementation. Here's the mapping of each claim to the code:\n\n## 1. Revealing how the workflow ran\n\n`workflow-log.js` fetches `/api/instances/:id/logs/sessions/:session` (`LogHandler.getSessionLogs`), which returns the full waterfall of one run: `levels` + `logs` (each row: `personaId?`, `level`, `agent`, `msg.parents`, input/output).\n\n- **Personas**: rows with `personaId != null` become node cards; workflow-scaffolding rows (`personaId: null` — the outer workflow *and* inner workflow frames) are filtered out, but the *personas of inner workflows* remain, so nested workflows' people are drawn too (`checkInnerPersonaFilter` pins this).\n- **Relations**: `drawEdges()` renders one bezier per `msg.parents` entry, so edges cross inner-workflow boundaries whenever both endpoints are rendered cards.\n- Layout is one column per level (`placeNodes`/`levelColumn`), with a mini-map grouped by level, duration-based speed tiers (green/blue/red), and a detail panel per persona (CONTEXT / INPUT / RESULT bubbles).\n\n## 2. Follow-ups on the workflow\n\nThe right panel's second, exclusive mode — the follow-up thread (`openFollowUpPanel`), auto-opened once on load when `sessionSuccess` is true, reopenable via the **Follow-up** button under the mini-map.\n\n- **Gating**: frontend requires *every* session log row `success`; backend enforces the same via `sessionRunEnd()` — a successful `run_ends` row is the source of truth (\"the workflow finished\").\n- **Footer state machine** (same grammar as graph.html): empty → *Seed original answer* (`POST .../seed`) → run in progress / summarizing → failed → *Retry* → success w/o summary → \"write a summary\" hint → success + summary → composer.\n- **Seed (run 0)**: materializes the original answer — prompt = the session's original ask, output = the tip persona's output — then a summarization pass; an empty summary lands as `failed`, never `answered`.\n- Turns POST `.../graph/sessions/:sid/run` with `{ prompt, parent_run_id: tip.id }`; per-turn delete, failed-tip retry, and manual summary edits (PUT `/graph/:nid/runs/:rid` with the session UUID as `:nid`) all reuse the node-RUN contracts.\n\n## 3. \"RUN item\" on the bottom-most persona\n\n`sessionTip()` derives the target: the persona log with **max `(level, startedAt)`** — the synthesized `_bottom_` completion or the single persona of the last level. Every follow-up re-executes *that* persona, with full fidelity (`sessionTipContext`):\n\n- **Agent**: re-resolved from the recorded agent name (`resolveSessionScript` — strip `openloop_`, project bin first, then user-local bin; base scripts and unknown agents → 400).\n- **Context files** written into the run dir: `ctx.md` (persona knowledge + MCPs collected across the instance→home chain), `convo.md` (thread summaries up to the parent), and one `parent-<id>.md` per parent with its full output — all referenced via `@`-prefixed lines in the exec prompt.\n- **Arg shape mirrors the runner's tip-persona invocation**: real personas get `[root, knowledgePath, prompt, about]`; the synthesized bottom gets the node-RUN 3-arg shape.\n- Turns are stored in `graph_run_logs` anchored on the **session UUID** (`node_id` is the overloaded anchor column; the `session_run` notification kind is a marker row written at insert time), with the identical lifecycle as node RUNs: `progress → summarizing → success | failed`, tip-only appends, failed-tip retry, splice-on-delete with reparenting.\n\nSo: same RUN engine, same conversation grammar as graph.html — the only differences are the anchor (session UUID instead of node id) and that the executor is persona-faithful (knowledge path + system prompt + parents' outputs) rather than the bare 3-arg node shape. This is also documented in `llms.txt` under \"Graph sessions\".\n"
